#: Passing.md:1
msgid "Passing data between Actions"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:3
msgid ""
"Each line in a PLUMED input file creates a PLMD::Action. Many of these "
"PLMD::Action commands calculate some quantity or some small set of "
"quantities. These quantities are stored in PLMD::Value objects. PLMD::Action"
" commands that calculate quantities thus inherit from the base class "
"PLMD::ActionWithValue. If a second PLMD::Action uses the PLMD::Value that is"
" calculated earlier in the input, then this section action will inherit from"
" PLMD::ActionWithArgument. To summarise:"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:9
msgid ""
"PLMD::Value holds the quantities that can be passed between PLMD::Action "
"objects."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:10
msgid "PLMD::ActionWithValue objects are used to set PLMD::Value objects."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:11
msgid "PLMD::ActionWithArguments objects are used to get PLMD::Value objects."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:13
msgid ""
"So does all the data that passes between PLMD::Action objects do so in "
"PLMD::Value objects? No."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:15
msgid ""
"Why not? PLMD::Value objects can only be used to pass scalars between "
"PLMD::Action objects. If one wishes to pass something more complicated (e.g."
" an atomic position), some alternative mechanism must be developed."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:18
msgid ""
"I have been interested in extending this mechanism for passing data between "
"Actions over the past few years. I wanted to develop a new version of the "
"PLMD::Value class that also allows one to pass vectors, matrices and "
"functions on grids between PLMD::Action objects. My interest in doing so "
"came initially from a desire to make it easy to pass all these quantities "
"from PLUMED to Python. I quickly realised, however, that the greater "
"flexibility this offers makes PLUMED more powerful."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:23
msgid "Anatomy of PLMD::Value"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:25
msgid ""
"The four most important variables in a PLUMED 2.9 PLMD::Value object are:"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:27
msgid "`value` = the value of the scalar"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:28
msgid ""
"`hasDeriv`= bool that is true if the action that calculates scalar also "
"calculates the derivatives of the value with respect to the input quantities"
" for the PLMD::Action that calculates it"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:29
msgid ""
"`derivatives` = the derivatives of the input quantity with respect to the "
"input quantities for the PLMD::Action that calculates it"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:30
msgid ""
"`inputForce` = the force that acts upon this scalar. In calculating the "
"forces on the atoms using the chain rule, this inputForce is multiplied by "
"the derivatives in `derivatives.`"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:32
msgid ""
"In the new version of PLUMED that allows one to pass scalars, vectors, "
"matrices and grids, these four variables are replaced by:"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:34
msgid ""
"`data` = a std::vector that contains all the data that is to be passed "
"between actions"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:35
msgid ""
"`shape` = a std::vector with 0 components if PLMD::Value is a scalar, 1 "
"component if PLMD::Value is a vector/1D grid, 2 components if PLMD::Value is"
" a matrix/2D grid."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:36
msgid ""
"`hasDeriv` = if shape.size()==0 this bool is true if the action that "
"calculates scalar also calculates the derivatives of the value with respect "
"to the input quantities for the PLMD::Action that calculates it. If "
"shape.size()>0, this bool is true if the PLMD::Value holds a function "
"computed on a grid."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:37
msgid ""
"`storedata` = a bool that is set true if hasDeriv is false, shape.size()>0, "
"and we need to store the values of the vector/matrix to calculate forces "
"using the chain rule."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:38
msgid ""
"`inputForce` = a std::vector that contains the forces that should be applied"
" on each component of the input object."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:40
msgid ""
"In future articles, I will write much more about the `storedata` variable "
"and how we can calculate forces __without__ storing derivatives with respect"
" to all the components. For now, though, I will explain the organisation of "
"the information in `data` the three most basic data types:"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:43
msgid ""
"__scalars without derivatives__ - the data vector has only one component, "
"which holds the scalar."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:44
msgid ""
"__scalar with derivatives__ - the data vector has 1 + number of derivatives "
"components. data[0] holds the value of the scalar. Every other component "
"holds a derivative of the scalar."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:45
msgid ""
"__vector__ - the data vector has `shape[0]` commponents. This is the number "
"of components in the vector."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:46
msgid ""
"__matrix__ - the data vector has `shape[0]*shape[1]` components. This is the"
" number of components in the matrix."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:48
msgid ""
"I will expand on what is done for matrices, and grids will be provided in "
"future articles as it is a little more complicated."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:50
msgid "Why/why not just scalars?"
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:52
msgid ""
"Systems in biochemistry, unlike those in condensed matter, do not have atom-"
"interchange symmetry. When designing CVs, there is thus no reason to say "
"that a quantity calculated for two different sets of atoms is "
"interchangeable. Therefore, in biochemistry (as opposed to materials "
"science), CVs are not often designed by calculating a vector of "
"symmetrically equivalent quantities and then performing some operation on "
"this vector (e.g. taking the average of all the quantities within it). "
"Simulators of biomolecules, for instance, would also rarely calculate a "
"radial distribution function, which is a ubiquitous operation among "
"researchers who simulate materials."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:57
msgid ""
"The majority of the simulations that are done with PLUMED are on systems of "
"biomolecules. For these simulations having fine-grained control over the "
"individual scalars passed in the input file makes sense, as these scalars "
"are not all symmetrically equivalent. You thus rarely encounter situations, "
"as you would in materials science, where the same operations should be done "
"for every scalar in a vector."
msgstr ""

#: Passing.md:61
msgid ""
"The introduction of methods such as parallel bias metadynamics suggests that"
" it is worth trying to change the way we introduce methods such as "
"metadynamics. This method was developed in 2018, 16 years after the "
"introduction of metadynamics. Given the time this development took, is it "
"worth doing more to raise awareness that averaging over symmetrically-"
"equivalent CVs is acceptable when using enhanced sampling methods?"
msgstr ""
